<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page5-continuous-games-stackelberg" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Continuous Games and Stackelberg's Duopoly</title>

  <p>
    So far, every game we've looked at has had a finite menu of choices at each
    node: send $80 or demand a receipt, cooperate or defect. What happens when a
    player can choose <em>any</em> real number instead? That's where calculus
    enters game theory, and it's the subject of this page.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-1-9">
    <title>Section 1.9, Continuous Games</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 1.9.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Pay attention to how backward induction gets adapted here. It's the same
      core idea as before, but instead of a finite number of nodes to compare,
      you have to consider values over an interval. We'll look at an example
      before moving on to the next section.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Let's look at an example. In the next section you'll read about
    <em>Stackelberg's model of duopoly</em>, which deals with two firms
    choosing how much of a good to produce, one right after the other. Firm 1
    picks its production level <m>s</m> first; then Firm 2 observes <m>s</m>
    and picks its own production level <m>t</m>. Each firm's profit,
    <m>\pi_1(s,t)</m> and <m>\pi_2(s,t)</m>, depends on <em>both</em>
    quantities together, since they're competing in the same market.
  </p>

  <p>
    Right now, we'll take the profit functions as given. In the book's
    numerical example they work out to
  </p>

  <md>
    <mrow>\pi_1(s,t) \amp = (16 - 2t)s - 2s^2,</mrow>
    <mrow>\pi_2(s,t) \amp = (16 - 2s)t - 2t^2.</mrow>
  </md>

  <p>
    Where those come from is exactly what the reading will cover, and we'll
    take a closer look at the formulas once you've read it. For now, notice the
    one feature that drives everything on this page: <alert>each firm's profit
    contains the <em>other</em> firm's production level.</alert> That's what
    makes this a game rather than two separate calculus problems.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since Firm 1 moves first, it has to reason about how Firm 2 will
    rationally respond to <em>every</em> production level Firm 1 could
    possibly choose. In other words, before Firm 1 picks anything, we need to
    find Firm 2's best-response function, <m>t = b(s)</m>: the value of
    <m>t</m> that maximizes Firm 2's profit, for each fixed <m>s</m>.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="eg-refresher-derivative-max">
    <title>Refresher: when does setting a derivative to zero find a maximum?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        We're about to maximize a function by setting a derivative equal to
        zero. Before we do: a critical point isn't automatically a maximum.
        What has to be true for that shortcut to be valid, and what does it
        mean to do this to a function of two variables?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Shape of the function.</alert> If the function is concave (its
        graph curves downward, like an upside-down parabola) over the whole
        domain, a critical point is guaranteed to be the single global
        maximum. Otherwise a critical point could instead be a minimum, an
        inflection point, or just one of several local maxima.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>The domain.</alert> If the domain has boundaries (a variable
        can't go below <m>0</m>, say), the true maximum might sit right at
        that boundary instead of at a point where the derivative is zero.
        Boundary points have to be checked separately in that case.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Two variables.</alert> For a function like <m>\pi_2(s,t)</m>,
        the same idea applies to the <alert>partial derivative</alert>:
        setting <m>\partial \pi_2/\partial t = 0</m> (holding <m>s</m> fixed)
        finds a critical point of the one-variable curve you get by slicing
        the surface at that particular <m>s</m>. Everything you know about
        one-variable max/min problems applies to that slice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both conditions hold for us here, which is why the work below is as
        short as it is. Keep them in mind anyway: when the model gets more
        realistic in Subsection 1.10.2, the domain grows a boundary and this
        stops being automatic.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    In the interactive below, drag the slider to set Firm 1's production level
    <m>s</m>. The left panel shows the profit surface <m>\pi_2(s,t)</m>,
    sliced at your chosen <m>s</m>; the right panel shows that same slice as a
    clean 2D curve of <m>\pi_2</m> versus <m>t</m>. Here the curve is concave
    and the domain is unrestricted, so the point where the tangent line is
    flat (<m>\partial \pi_2/\partial t = 0</m>) is guaranteed to be the actual
    maximum, no boundary check needed. That peak is Firm 2's best response,
    <m>b(s)</m>, to your chosen <m>s</m>.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-firm2-bestresponse">
    <caption>
      Firm 2's profit as a function of <m>t</m>, for a fixed <m>s</m> you
      control. The left panel shows where this curve comes from as a slice of
      the full profit surface; the right panel shows the slice itself, with
      the maximum, its flat tangent line, and <m>b(s)</m> labeled.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-firm2-bestresponse"
      iframe="ch1-graph/firm2-best-response.html" width="100%" aspect="45:32">
      <static>
        <p>
          A slider for Firm 1's production level s, ranging from 0 to 10 in
          increments of 0.5. A 3D surface plot of pi_2(s,t) on the left,
          highlighting the curve where the surface is sliced at the current
          s. A 2D plot on the right of that same slice, pi_2 versus t, with
          the maximum point marked, a flat tangent line drawn at the maximum
          labeled "d(pi_2)/dt = 0", and the corresponding t-value labeled "t
          = b(s)".
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Watching the peak slide as you drag <m>s</m> is the whole content of the
    best-response function: every position of the slider gives one value of
    <m>b(s)</m>. Here's that same argument done with algebra instead of a
    slider. Try it yourself first if you'd like.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="eg-derive-best-response">
    <title>Deriving Firm 2's best response <m>b(s)</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Hold <m>s</m> fixed and find the value of <m>t</m> that maximizes
        <m>\pi_2(s,t) = (16 - 2s)t - 2t^2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        With <m>s</m> held fixed, this is a quadratic in <m>t</m> whose
        <m>t^2</m> coefficient is <m>-2</m>, so its graph is an upside-down
        parabola no matter what <m>s</m> is. By the refresher above, a
        critical point is therefore the global maximum, and we can find it by
        setting the partial derivative equal to zero:
      </p>
      <me>\frac{\partial \pi_2}{\partial t} = 16 - 2s - 4t = 0.</me>
      <p>
        Solving for <m>t</m> gives Firm 2's best-response function:
      </p>
      <me>t = b(s) = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s.</me>
      <p>
        This is a <em>strategy</em>, not a single move: it tells Firm 2 what
        to do at every node it could find itself at. Check it against the
        interactive by setting the slider to <m>s = 4</m> and confirming the
        peak sits at <m>t = 2</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    Now that Firm 2's best response is pinned down for every possible
    <m>s</m>, Firm 1 can fold that entire strategy into its own decision. Firm
    1's actual payoff, once Firm 2 responds optimally, is
    <m>\pi_1(s, b(s))</m>, a function of <m>s</m> alone. Substituting
    <m>b(s) = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s</m> into <m>\pi_1(s,t) = (16 - 2t)s - 2s^2</m>
    gives
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_1\bigl(s, b(s)\bigr)
      = \left(16 - 2\left(4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s\right)\right)s - 2s^2
      = 8s - s^2.
  </me>

  <p>
    That is the function graphed below. Unlike Firm 2's problem, there's no
    second variable left to slice away here, so this graph is just a single 2D
    curve: no 3D backdrop needed, since there's nothing being sliced.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-firm1-bestresponse">
    <caption>
      Firm 1's payoff <m>\pi_1(s, b(s)) = 8s - s^2</m> as a function of
      <m>s</m>, once Firm 2's best response is accounted for.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-firm1-bestresponse"
      iframe="ch1-graph/firm1-best-response.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
      <static>
        <p>
          A slider lets you set s from 0 to 10. A 2D plot of pi_1(s, b(s))
          versus s shows a point sweeping along the curve as you move the
          slider, alongside a fixed marker at the maximum. The maximum point
          is marked, with a flat tangent line at the maximum labeled
          "d(pi_1)/ds = 0", and the corresponding s-value labeled "s = s*".
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The slider shows you roughly where the peak sits. Here is how to pin it
    down exactly.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="eg-derive-firm1-optimum">
    <title>Finding Firm 1's optimal production level <m>s^*</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Maximize <m>\pi_1(s, b(s)) = 8s - s^2</m>, then work out what both
        firms actually produce and earn.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The <m>s^2</m> coefficient is negative, so this is an upside-down
        parabola and the derivative test suffices:
      </p>
      <me>\frac{d}{ds}\,\pi_1\bigl(s, b(s)\bigr) = 8 - 2s = 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad s^* = 4.</me>
      <p>
        Firm 2 then responds with <m>t^* = b(4) = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(4) = 2</m>,
        the price is <m>p^* = 20 - 2(4 + 2) = 8</m>, and the profits are
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\pi_1(s^*,t^*) \amp = (16 - 2 \cdot 2)4 - 2 \cdot 4^2 = 16,</mrow>
        <mrow>\pi_2(s^*,t^*) \amp = (16 - 2 \cdot 4)2 - 2 \cdot 2^2 = 8.</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        Firm 1 produces twice as much and earns twice as much. <alert>It is
        better to be the firm that moves first.</alert> That's worth pausing
        on, since in the Ultimatum Game moving first was also an advantage,
        but for a completely different reason.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    You've now walked through the entire backward induction argument:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        Solve Firm 2's problem first, giving the best-response function
        <m>b(s)</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Then write out <m>\pi_1(s, b(s))</m>, the payoff function for Firm 1
        assuming Firm 2 will always respond rationally to each <m>s</m> value.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Last, find the <m>s^*</m> that maximizes <m>\pi_1(s, b(s))</m>. Then
        you know Firm 1 chooses <m>s^*</m>, and Firm 2 chooses
        <m>t = b(s^*)</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    What we haven't done is say where <m>\pi_1</m> and <m>\pi_2</m> came from
    in the first place. That's the reading.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-1-10-1">
    <title>Section 1.10 through Subsection 1.10.1, Stackelberg's Model of Duopoly</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 1.10 up through Subsection 1.10.1. Stop before
      Subsection 1.10.2.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The backward induction in 1.10.1 is the argument we just did, so you can
      move through it quickly and use it to check your understanding. What's
      genuinely new is the setup <em>before</em> the calculus starts: the
      price rule, the cost functions, and how those combine into <m>\pi_1</m>
      and <m>\pi_2</m>. Read that part slowly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice also that the domain here is left unrestricted on purpose:
      production levels are allowed to be any real numbers, even negative
      ones. That's not realistic, and the book says so. It's a deliberate
      simplification that keeps the concavity argument clean, with no boundary
      to check.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Now let's take the profit functions apart. Every Stackelberg-type model in
    this chapter is built from the same four moves, and naming them makes the
    more general version in Section 1.11 feel like review:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Price rule.</alert> Total production is <m>q = s + t</m>, and
        price falls as <m>q</m> rises: <m>p = 20 - 2(s+t)</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Revenue.</alert> Each firm sells its own quantity at that
        shared market price: <m>ps</m> for Firm 1, <m>pt</m> for Firm 2.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Cost.</alert> Both firms have unit cost <m>4</m>, so
        <m>c_1(s) = 4s</m> and <m>c_2(t) = 4t</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><alert>Profit is revenue minus cost.</alert></p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Assembling those for Firm 1:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_1(s,t) \;=\;
    \underbrace{\underbrace{\bigl[\,20 - 2(s+t)\,\bigr]}_{\text{price } p}
    \cdot \underbrace{s}_{\substack{\text{Firm 1's} \\ \text{quantity}}}}_{\text{revenue}}
    \;-\; \underbrace{4s}_{\text{cost } c_1(s)}
    \;=\; (16 - 2t)s - 2s^2.
  </me>

  <p>
    Firm 2's is the mirror image, with <m>t</m> in the role of <m>s</m>:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_2(s,t) \;=\; \bigl[\,20 - 2(s+t)\,\bigr]t - 4t \;=\; (16 - 2s)t - 2t^2.
  </me>

  <p>
    The single most important thing to see here is that <m>s</m> and <m>t</m>
    enter the price rule together, as a sum. Neither firm controls the price;
    they control it jointly, and that's the only channel through which one
    firm's decision touches the other's profit.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-build-profit-functions">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose we rerun the model with different numbers. Price falls
        according to <m>p = 30 - 3(s+t)</m>, and both firms now have a unit
        cost of production of <m>6</m>. Which of the following is Firm 2's
        profit function, <em>before</em> any simplifying?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_2(s,t) = \bigl[30 - 3(s+t)\bigr]t - 6t</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Price times Firm 2's <em>own</em> quantity, minus Firm
            2's <em>own</em> cost. The rival's production <m>s</m> shows up
            only inside the price, which is exactly the structure of the
            original model.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_2(s,t) = \bigl[30 - 3(s+t)\bigr](s+t) - 6t</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. This charges Firm 2 for its own production but credits
            it with revenue from <em>everything</em> both firms sold. Firm 2
            only earns money on the <m>t</m> units it produced itself.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_2(s,t) = \bigl[30 - 3t\bigr]t - 6t</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Dropping <m>s</m> from the price rule says Firm 1's
            production has no effect on the market price. If that were true
            there'd be no game here at all, just two unrelated optimization
            problems.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_2(s,t) = \bigl[30 - 3(s+t)\bigr]t - 6s</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite, check the cost term. Firm 2 pays to produce its own
            <m>t</m> units; Firm 1's production bill is Firm 1's problem.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-why-t-in-pi1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Firm 1 never chooses <m>t</m>. So why does <m>t</m> appear in Firm 1's
        profit function <m>\pi_1(s,t) = (16-2t)s - 2s^2</m> at all?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Both firms sell into the same market at a single price, and that
          price depends on the total quantity <m>s + t</m>. More production by
          Firm 2 pushes the price down, which lowers what Firm 1 earns on
          every unit it sells.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the whole reason backward induction is needed
            here. If <m>t</m> didn't appear in <m>\pi_1</m>, Firm 1 could just
            maximize its own profit directly and ignore Firm 2 entirely.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Because Firm 1 has to pay part of Firm 2's production costs.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Each firm pays only its own costs: <m>c_1(s) = 4s</m>
            for Firm 1 and <m>c_2(t) = 4t</m> for Firm 2. The link between the
            firms runs through the price, not through costs.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Because Firm 1 moves first, so it has to commit to a value of
          <m>t</m> on Firm 2's behalf.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Firm 1 only chooses <m>s</m>. It <em>anticipates</em>
            <m>t</m> through the best-response function <m>b(s)</m>, but it
            never chooses <m>t</m>, and <m>t</m> would sit in <m>\pi_1</m>
            even if the two firms moved simultaneously.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-concavity-slice">
    <statement>
      <p>
        To find <m>b(s)</m>, we held <m>s</m> fixed and set
        <m>\partial \pi_2/\partial t = 0</m>, then stopped. What justifies
        stopping there, rather than doing more work to confirm we found a
        maximum?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>For any fixed <m>s</m>, the function
          <m>\pi_2 = (16-2s)t - 2t^2</m> is a quadratic in <m>t</m> with a
          negative <m>t^2</m> coefficient, so every slice is an upside-down
          parabola with one global maximum, and <m>t</m> ranges over all real
          numbers, so there's no boundary to check.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Both conditions from the refresher hold, and notice the
            <m>t^2</m> coefficient is <m>-2</m> regardless of <m>s</m>, so
            this argument covers every slice at once, not just the one
            currently on your screen.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Setting a derivative equal to zero always finds a maximum.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. It finds a critical point, which could be a minimum or
            an inflection point. We need the concavity to know which one we
            got.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The interactive shows a peak, so we can see the maximum is
          there.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The picture is good evidence, but it only shows finitely many
            values of <m>s</m> over a limited window. The concavity argument
            is what covers <em>every</em> <m>s</m> at once.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    With <m>\pi_1</m> and <m>\pi_2</m> understood, the next step is to make
    the model more realistic by refusing to let production and price go
    negative. That's Subsection 1.10.2, and it's where the boundary case from
    the refresher finally earns its keep.
  </p>

 <!-- ==================================================================
     1.10.2 BLOCK, v3.
     Order now follows the book: the s >= 8 case is settled first, and
     the simplified formula appears only for the s < 8 case, where it is
     actually used.

     Needs: assets/ch1-graph/firm2-profitable-window.html
     ================================================================== -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-1-10-2">
    <title>Subsection 1.10.2, The Second Model</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 1.10 (that is, Subsection 1.10.2).</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The first model let production levels and prices go negative, which
      isn't something that happens in an actual market. This subsection fixes
      that. As you read, keep track of what the fix costs us mathematically:
      the profit functions stop being single formulas and become piecewise.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The general procedure is the same, but there are some nuanced points now. 
    Some of the reasoning is hard to follow in the book, so we will break it down a bit more here. 
    Here is Firm 1's, labeled:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_1(s,t) =
    \begin{cases}
      \overbrace{\bigl[20 - 2(s+t)\bigr]s - 4s}^{\substack{\text{exactly the payoff from the first model:} \\ \text{price is still } 20 - 2(s+t) \text{ in this range}}}
        \amp \text{if } 0 \leq s+t \lt 10, \\[4ex]
      \underbrace{-4s}_{\substack{\text{price has bottomed out at } 0, \text{ so revenue is } 0 \cdot s = 0; \\ \text{profit is } 0 - 4s, \text{ a pure loss of the production cost}}}
        \amp \text{if } s+t \geq 10.
    \end{cases}
  </me>

  <p>
    Firm 2's is the mirror image, with <m>t</m> in the role of <m>s</m>:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_2(s,t) =
    \begin{cases}
      \bigl[20 - 2(s+t)\bigr]t - 4t \amp \text{if } 0 \leq s+t \lt 10, \\[1ex]
      -4t \amp \text{if } s+t \geq 10.
    \end{cases}
  </me>

  <p>
    The book brushes over some of the reasoning, so 
    let's take a closer look at Firm 2's pay-off function. Drag the slider below. The
    shaded region marks the production levels that actually earn Firm 2 a
    profit. Push <m>s</m> upward and watch what happens to it.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-firm2-profitable-window">
    <caption>
      Firm 2's profit in the second model, for a value of <m>s</m> you
      control. The shaded region is where profit is positive; the marker is
      Firm 2's best response.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-firm2-profitable-window"
      iframe="ch1-graph/firm2-profitable-window.html" width="100%" aspect="10:9">
      <static>
        <p>
          A slider sets Firm 1's production level s from 0 to 10. A plot of
          Firm 2's profit against its own production t shows a downward
          parabola that becomes the line -4t once s + t reaches 10. The
          region where profit is positive, running from t = 0 to t = 8 - s,
          is shaded, and it narrows as s increases until it disappears
          entirely at s = 8. A marker tracks Firm 2's best response, which
          sits at the peak of the parabola while one exists and jumps to
          t = 0 once s reaches 8.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <alert>Notice what happens once you set <m> s <8 </m> vs <m>s \geq 8</m></alert>:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          When <m> s <8 </m>: Firm 2 is profitable when <m> 0< s+t < 10 </m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>s <m>s \geq 8</m>: The shaded region is gone: there is no production level
    left that earns Firm 2 a profit. 
        </li>
      </ul> 
  </p>



<p> The book gives a brief explanation on why this happens, which we will examine in a bit. But, for now, 
see if you can use the above observation to determine Firm 2's best response in the following scenarios: </p>

 <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-s9">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Firm 1 produces <m>s = 9</m>. What is Firm 2's best response?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 0</m>. Since <m>9 \geq 8</m>, Firm 2's profit on each unit
          is <m>16 - 18 - 2t = -2 - 2t</m>, negative for every <m>t</m>. Every
          unit loses money, so producing nothing and earning <m>0</m> is the
          best available outcome.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Firm 2 isn't producing nothing as a strategic move, it's
            producing nothing because every alternative loses money.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(9) = -0.5</m>, from the best-response
          formula.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This applies the <m>s \lt 8</m> formula outside the range where
            it's valid, and negative production is exactly what the second
            model rules out. It is, though, a good way to see why the second
            case exists: when the parabola's peak lands at a negative
            <m>t</m>, the best allowed choice is the nearest point of the
            domain, <m>t = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 4</m>, the largest value the best-response function
          takes.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            <m>t = 4</m> is <m>b(0)</m>, Firm 2's best response when Firm 1
            produces <em>nothing</em>. That's the opposite end of the range
            from <m>s = 9</m>. Check which case <m>s = 9</m> falls into before
            picking a value of <m>t</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-s7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Now Firm 1 produces <m>s = 7</m>. What is Firm 2's best response?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 0.5</m>, since <m>7 \lt 8</m> puts us in the first case,
          where <m>b(s) = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s = 4 - 3.5</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The profitable window here is narrow, running only from
            <m>t = 0</m> to <m>t = 8 - 7 = 1</m>, but it isn't empty, and
            Firm 2's best response sits right in the middle of it. Set the
            slider to <m>7</m> and compare.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 0</m>, since <m>s = 7</m> is close enough to <m>8</m> that
          the price is already at Firm 2's cost.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close isn't the same as reaching it. At <m>s = 7</m> with Firm 2
            producing nothing, the price is <m>20 - 14 = 6</m>, still above
            the cost of <m>4</m>. There's a small profit to be had, and Firm
            2 takes it. The corner solution only kicks in at <m>s = 8</m>
            exactly.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 1</m>, the right-hand edge of the profitable region.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            <m>t = 8 - s = 1</m> is where profit falls back <em>to</em> zero,
            not where it's largest. Producing there earns Firm 2 nothing at
            all. The maximum is at the peak of the parabola, halfway between
            the two places profit equals zero.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's mathematically justify the two cases (as the book very briefly explains why). 
    Look at <m>\pi_2</m> for <m>0 \leq t < 10-s </m>, and factor the <m>t</m> out of it
    instead of multiplying everything through:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_2(s,t) \;=\;
    \underbrace{\bigl[20 - 2(s+t)\bigr]}_{\text{price } p} t
    \;-\; \underbrace{4}_{\substack{\text{unit} \\ \text{cost}}} t
    \;=\;
    \underbrace{\bigl[\,20 - 2(s+t) - 4\,\bigr]}_{\text{profit on each unit sold}}
    \cdot \underbrace{t}_{\substack{\text{units} \\ \text{sold}}}.
  </me>

  <p>
    Written this way, Firm 2's profit is just <em>profit per unit</em> times
    <em>number of units</em>. Now suppose <m>s \geq 8</m>. Then
  </p>

  <me>
    \underbrace{20 - 2(s+t) - 4}_{\text{profit on each unit}}
      \;=\; 16 - 2s - 2t
      \;\leq\; 16 - 16 - 2t
      \;=\; -2t \;\leq\; 0.
  </me>

  <p>
    So Firm 2 loses money on every unit it makes, and producing <em>more</em>
    only pushes the price further below cost, making the per-unit loss worse.
    The best Firm 2 can do is produce nothing
    and break even at <m>0</m>.
  </p>

 

  <p>
    <alert>In contrast, when <m>s \lt 8</m></alert>, the price stays above
    <m>4</m> and Firm 2 does have room to profit. Multiplying out the top
    piece and substituting the fixed <m>s</m> gives the function the book
    graphs in Figure 1.11:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_2(s,t) =
    \begin{cases}
      (16 - 2s)t - 2t^2 \amp \text{if } 0 \leq t \lt 10 - s, \\[1ex]
      -4t \amp \text{if } t \geq 10 - s.
    \end{cases}
  </me>

  <p>
    That top piece is an upside-down parabola, so the derivative test applies
    to it exactly as it did in the first model, and the maximum sits at
    <m>t = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s</m>.
  </p>

 

  <p>
    Putting the two cases together gives Firm 2's best-response function:
  </p>

  <me>
    b(s) =
    \begin{cases}
      \overbrace{4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s}^{\substack{\text{the peak of the parabola: the same best} \\ \text{response as in the first model}}}
        \amp \text{if } 0 \leq s \lt 8, \\[5ex]
      \underbrace{0}_{\substack{\text{no profitable production level exists, since every } t \gt 0 \\ \text{loses money, so Firm 2 produces nothing and breaks even}}}
        \amp \text{if } s \geq 8.
    \end{cases}
  </me>

  <p>
    That second case is a <alert>corner solution</alert>: the best choice sits
    at the edge of the domain rather than where a derivative vanishes. It's
    the first one in this chapter.
  </p>

  <p>
    Firm 1 never wants <m>s \geq 8</m> anyway, since that pushes the price to
    its own cost of production or below. And on the range it does care about,
    the piecewise complication never bites: for <m>0 \leq s \lt 8</m>,
    <m>s + b(s) = 4 + \tfrac{1}{2}s \lt 8 \lt 10</m>, so <m>\pi_1</m> stays on
    its first piece and <m>\pi_1(s, b(s)) = 8s - s^2</m> exactly as before.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-stackelberg-second-model">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Putting it together: does restricting production and price to be
        nonnegative change the equilibrium production levels or profits?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>No. The optimal <m>s^* = 4</m> was already comfortably below the
          threshold of <m>8</m>, so the new boundary never comes into
          play.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The boundary would only have mattered if the
            unconstrained optimum had pushed production or price into
            negative territory. We get the same <m>t^* = 2</m>,
            <m>p^* = 8</m>, and profits of <m>16</m> and <m>8</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Yes, Firm 1's profit changes because it now has to account for
          the possibility of a zero price.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Firm 1's optimal choice never comes close to the
            region where the price would hit zero, so nothing about the
            profit calculation changes.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Yes, Firm 2 now produces nothing, since negative production is
          no longer allowed.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Producing nothing is Firm 2's best response only when
            <m>s \geq 8</m>. At the equilibrium <m>s^* = 4</m>, Firm 2's best
            response is <m>t^* = 2</m>, so the restriction doesn't bind.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Worth carrying forward from this section:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>The model changed; the method didn't.</alert> Piecewise
        payoffs mean extra work, not different work: each piece gets
        examined, and the boundary <m>t = 0</m> gets checked separately,
        because a derivative can't see it.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Profit is margin times quantity.</alert> Factoring
        <m>\pi_2</m> as <m>\bigl[p - 4\bigr]t</m> rather than multiplying it
        out is what makes the <m>s \geq 8</m> case a one-line argument.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Two thresholds, two jobs.</alert> <m>s = 8</m> is where the
        price meets Firm 2's <em>cost</em>, which is what closes the
        profitable window. <m>s + t = 10</m> is where the price hits
        <em>zero</em>, which is where the payoff switches pieces.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>The answer didn't move.</alert> Still <m>s^* = 4</m>,
        <m>t^* = 2</m>, <m>p^* = 8</m>, profits <m>16</m> and <m>8</m>.
        Realism cost us nothing here, because the optimum was never near the
        boundary.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>A question to carry forward.</alert> That held for these
        particular numbers. Would it hold for a different price rule or cost?
        Section 1.11 redoes all of this with symbols, which is what lets you
        answer that.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

</section>
